course: dictionary
lesson: 3814
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. You can tell which meaning is meant by reading the words around it. Forge is a good example: it can be a place, a thing you do with metal, or a crime.
explain: «The blacksmith worked all day at the forge.» Here a forge is a workshop with a very hot fire, where metal is heated and shaped. Forge is a noun, the name of a place.
explain: «Ollie forged a sick note to skip swimming.» Now forged is a verb. The clue is note: to forge something is to make a fake copy to trick people, and that is a crime. Check by putting your meaning into the sentence. Does «Ollie made a fake copy of a sick note» still make sense? It does.
explain: «The runners forged ahead in the last mile.» Forge ahead means move forward strongly. No metal is being heated here, so the clue is the direction: ahead. Context tells you the meaning has changed.
fact: Forge can be a noun (a workshop) or a verb (to forge, to make or shape).
fact: «Forge» means to heat metal and hammer it into shape.
fact: «Forge a signature» means to copy someone's name to trick people, and it is a crime.
fact: «Forge ahead» means to move forward strongly.
fact: «Forge a friendship» means to build one with effort.
exercise: «The blacksmith heated the iron in the forge.» What is a forge here? | *A workshop with a very hot fire | A friendly talk | A kind of horse | A fake copy of a document
exercise: «A forged signature on a school note is a serious lie.» What does forged mean here? | *Made as a fake copy to trick people | Written in neat joined-up writing | Finished early | Heated and hammered into shape
exercise: «Ollie wanted to forge ahead with his swimming, not stop.» What does forge ahead mean? | Heat metal in a fire | Go backwards slowly | *Move forward strongly | Copy someone's name
test: «Ella and Sam worked hard to forge a friendship.» What does forge mean here? | Break it quickly | *Make it with effort | Heat it in a fire | Copy it to trick people
test: Why is forging a parent's signature a serious problem? | It is kind, because it saves the parent a job | It makes the signature look neater | *It is a dishonest lie that pretends the parent agreed | It is only a spelling mistake
exam: In «The smith lit the forge, and then began to forge the iron», what is forge in each part? | Verb in both parts | Noun in both parts | *Noun in the first part, verb in the second | Verb in the first part, noun in the second
